\begin{proposition}[Torus fibres of Euler numbers $-2$ and zero]\hypertarget{tag-01FH}{}\label{cdp:st:C2b}
\StatementRecord{cdp:st:C2b}{proved}{01FH}{}

There is a smooth projective threefold $Y$ with a proper connected-fibre map $Y\to\PP^1$, smooth general fibre an abelian surface, and a reduced normal-crossing fibre of Euler number $-2$. There is also such a threefold with a singular reduced fibre of Euler number zero whose three irreducible components are a blown-up abelian surface, a ruled surface over a genus-two curve, and $\PP^2$.
\end{proposition}


\begin{proof}

\begin{enumerate}[label=\arabic*.,leftmargin=*]
\item Let $C_2$ be the smooth projective completion of $w^2=z^6-1$. Its six simple branch points and the unramified points at infinity give genus two by Riemann--Hurwitz. Let $A=\operatorname{Jac}(C_2)$ and embed $C_2$ in $A$ by $p\mapsto\OO(p-p_*)$. This is injective because a principal divisor $p-q$ for distinct points would define a degree-one map $C_2\to\PP^1$. It is an immersion because the canonical system of a genus-two curve has no base point; the explicit differentials $dz/w,z\,dz/w$ verify that property, including at infinity. A proper injective immersion is an embedding. Adjunction on the torus gives $N_{C_2/A}=K_{C_2}$.

\item Blow up $A\times\PP^1$ along $C_2\times\{0\}$, and denote the resulting smooth projective threefold by $Y$. Compose its blowdown with projection to $\PP^1$. The exceptional divisor is $E=\PP(K_{C_2}\oplus\OO_{C_2})$. The strict transform of $A\times\{0\}$ is $A$ itself, since blowing up a Cartier divisor in a smooth surface is an isomorphism. These two components meet along a section isomorphic to $C_2$. In local coordinates $(u,v,t)$ with centre $(u,t)=(0,0)$, a blowup chart has $t=u\tau$, so the fibre is the reduced normal-crossing divisor $u\tau=0$. The other chart and the complement give the claimed global map and connectedness of its fibres. Euler additivity gives
\[
 e((Y_0)_{\rm red})=e(A)+e(E)-e(C_2)=0+2(-2)-(-2)=-2.
\]
Every other fibre is $A$.

\item Now blow up one point of the strict-transform component $A$ away from $C_2$. The new central fibre has components $\operatorname{Bl}_pA$, $E$, and $\PP^2$. Its pairwise intersections are $C_2$ and $\PP^1$, with no triple intersection. Its Euler number is
\[
1-4+3-(-2)-2=0.
\]
Both modifications have smooth total space and reduced normal-crossing central fibre. For completeness the integral cohomology is torsion-free. The Betti numbers of $A\times\PP^1$ are $(1,4,7,8,7,4,1)$; the blowup formula adds $H^{k-2}(C_2,\Z)$ in degree $k$, giving $(1,4,8,12,8,4,1)$. The point blowup adds one generator in degrees two and four, giving $(1,4,9,12,9,4,1)$. These formulas follow from excision for the exceptional projective bundle and its hyperplane class, whose restriction identifies the new relative summands. Their Euler numbers are respectively $-2$ and zero, agreeing with the fibre computation.

\end{enumerate}
\end{proof}

