total space compact. Base change of a proper holomorphic
submersion preserves both properties; the base-changed total
space is compact because its base is again $\PP^1$.
 For $p=(0,1)$, this gives
\begin{equation}
 V_z^{-1}\bigl(iV_z(p)\bigr)
       =\frac{i(2z,1-r^2)}{1+r^2}.
 \hypertarget{tag-013U}{}\label{cdp:eq:C.TL.5}
\end{equation}
The first three original periods span $\C\times\R$, and their real determinant with a fourth vector $v$ is $C\operatorname{Im}v^{(2)}$. Put $r=|z|$ and $\tau=N\log z/(2\pi i)$. The contribution $\operatorname{Re}\tau\,V_z(p)$ does not change the determinant. The imaginary contribution uses \eqref{cdp:eq:C.TL.5}, together with $\operatorname{Im}\tau=-N\log r/(2\pi)$ and $\operatorname{Im}b^{(2)}=1$. Multiplication by the real determinant of $V_z$ gives exactly \eqref{cdp:eq:C.TL.6}. It is positive because $(r^2-1)\log r\ge0$.

For the power-$R$ family make the same determinant calculation
with the real-linear map $V_{z^R}$ and
$\tau=N\log z/(2\pi i)$. Its inverse and determinant are the
preceding ones at $z^R$; in particular the second coordinate of
$V_{z^R}^{-1}(iV_{z^R}p)$ is $i(1-r^{2R})/(1+r^{2R})$.
The same fourth-column calculation gives
\eqref{eq:II:logarithmic-power-determinant}. It is positive because
$(r^{2R}-1)\log r\ge0$. Division by $z^R$ gives its displayed
infinity frame and the same negative logarithmic coefficient.

For either family, on a simply connected base chart the four holomorphic periods act by translations on $\C^2$. Their real matrices and inverses are uniformly bounded over every compact base subset. Only finitely many integral translation vectors can move one prescribed compact subset into another, so the action is properly discontinuous. The continuously varying closed real parallelepipeds give properness of the quotient over that base subset. The integral change $P_4\mapsto P_4+NP_3$ identifies these quotients on changes of logarithm, constructing the stated family on all of $\C^*$.

Division of each period by $z$ gives \eqref{cdp:eq:C.TL.7}. Since $\log z=-\log s$ up to $2\pi i\Z$, the fourth period becomes the fourth vector in \eqref{cdp:eq:C.TL.8}, with precisely its negative logarithmic coefficient. Its branch changes again add an integral period. This proves the full frame and period comparison at infinity.
\end{proof}

old_text:
\begin{lemma}[Logarithmic period families with a finite root action]\hypertarget{tag-P358}{}\label{lem:II:finite-equivariant-periods}
\StatementRecord{lem:II:finite-equivariant-periods}{proved}{P358}{\cref{lem:II:weighted-cycle-affine-data,lem:II:logarithmic-period-family}}
Use the full lattice and affine data of
\cref{lem:II:weighted-cycle-affine-data}, with
$b=(\sigma_0,i)$ and $R=\operatorname{diag}(\rho,1)$.
Choose $1\le k\le N$ with $\rho=\zeta_N^k$, taking $k=N$
when $\rho=1$. Put
\begin{equation}
 J=\begin{pmatrix}0&1\\-1&0\end{pmatrix},\qquad
 V_s(w)=w+s^kJ\overline w.
\hypertarget{tag-00K0}{}\label{cdp:eq:C.EW.8}
\end{equation}
Then
\begin{equation}
                           R V_s=V_{\zeta_Ns}R.
\hypertarget{tag-00K1}{}\label{cdp:eq:C.EW.9}
\end{equation}
and the periods
\begin{equation}
 \begin{gathered}
 \Lambda_s=V_s\Lambda,\qquad
 B_s=V_s b+\frac{d\log s}{2\pi i}V_s p,\\
                         T_s=\C^2/(\Lambda_s+\Z B_s).
 \end{gathered}
\hypertarget{tag-00JI}{}\label{cdp:eq:C.EW.10}
\end{equation}
define a smooth proper family of connected two-tori on
$\C^*_s$. In the real coordinate order of
\cref{lem:II:logarithmic-period-family}, its determinant is
\begin{equation}
 C(1+r_s^{2k})
 \left(1+r_s^{2k}
       +\frac d{2\pi}(r_s^{2k}-1)\log r_s\right)>0.
\hypertarget{tag-00JJ}{}\label{cdp:eq:C.EW.11}
\end{equation}
where $r_s=|s|$ and $C$ is the oriented lattice constant in
\eqref{cdp:eq:C.EW.5}.

Transport the full edge and action data by
\begin{equation}
 \begin{aligned}
 \mathbf b_j(s)&=V_s \mathbf b_j,& n_j(s)&=V_s n_j,\\
 l_j(s)&=-h_jn_j(s)/(2\pi i),&
 \tau_j(s)&=V_{\zeta_Ns}\tau_j.
 \end{aligned}
\hypertarget{tag-00JK}{}\label{cdp:eq:C.EW.12}
\end{equation}
Their overlap identity is
\begin{equation}
 \begin{aligned}
 &\tau_{j+1}(s)+R\mathbf b_j(s)-\tau_j(s)-\mathbf b_{\sigma j}(\zeta_Ns)\\
 &\qquad-l_{\sigma j}(\zeta_Ns)\,2\pi i/N
       =V_{\zeta_Ns}\lambda_j.
 \end{aligned}
\hypertarget{tag-00JL}{}\label{cdp:eq:C.EW.13}
\end{equation}
and their $N$fold action has translation $V_s\mu_j$.
The last period satisfies
\begin{equation}
                    R B_s-B_{\zeta_Ns}
                   =V_{\zeta_Ns}\bigl((R-I)b-u/N\bigr)
                   \in\Lambda_{\zeta_Ns}.
\hypertarget{tag-00JM}{}\label{cdp:eq:C.EW.14}
\end{equation}
Thus they define a holomorphic finite action on the entire
punctured family. More precisely, set
$\theta_j(s)=\sum_{a<j}(\mathbf b_a(s)+l_a(s)\log s)$.
In the common coordinate $v_j-\theta_j(s)$ its translation is
\begin{equation}
 R\theta_j(s)+\tau_j(s)-\theta_{\sigma j}(\zeta_Ns).
\hypertarget{tag-00JN}{}\label{cdp:eq:C.EW.15}
\end{equation}

At infinity put $w=s^{-1}$ and divide the vector frame by
$s^k$. Then
\begin{equation}
 W_w(c)=J\overline c+w^k c.
\hypertarget{tag-00JQ}{}\label{cdp:eq:C.EW.18}
\end{equation}
and the periods are $W_w\Lambda$ and
\begin{equation}
                    W_wb-\frac{d\log w}{2\pi i}W_wp.
\hypertarget{tag-00JR}{}\label{cdp:eq:C.EW.19}
\end{equation}
The transformed finite action is exactly
\begin{equation}
 \begin{aligned}
 (j,v,w)&\longmapsto
     (\sigma j,R_\infty v+\tau_{j,\infty}(w),\zeta_N^{-1}w),\\
 R_\infty&=\rho^{-1}R=J\overline R J^{-1},\\
 \tau_{j,\infty}(w)
     &=J\overline{\tau_j}+\rho^{-1}w^k\tau_j
       =W_{\zeta_N^{-1}w}\tau_j.
 \end{aligned}
\hypertarget{tag-00JT}{}\label{cdp:eq:C.EW.20}
\end{equation}
At $w=0$ its lattice and affine data are the conjugates of
the data at zero through $v\mapsto J\overline v$ and the
conjugate base rotation.
\end{lemma}
\begin{proof}
For fixed $c$, $V_s c$ is holomorphic in $s$. The identity
$RJ\overline R^{-1}=\rho J$ gives
\eqref{cdp:eq:C.EW.9}. The map $V_s$ is the map $V_z$ of
\cref{lem:II:logarithmic-period-family} at $z=s^k$; its
displayed norm identity and inverse therefore apply to the
same real vector space and every period vector.
For the fourth period take the logarithmic coefficient
$d\log s/(2\pi i)$. On a logarithm chart this is
$(d/k)\log z/(2\pi i)$. The determinant identity
\eqref{cdp:eq:C.TL.6}, with its coefficient denoted by $c$,
is affine in $c$ on both sides and is proved for every positive
integer $c$. It is consequently the same polynomial identity
at $c=d/k$.
Substituting $z=s^k$ gives exactly
\eqref{cdp:eq:C.EW.11}, with no change of real coordinates.
It is positive since $(r_s^{2k}-1)\log r_s\ge0$.
Logarithm monodromy adds $dV_sp\in\Lambda_s$. Uniformly
bounded period matrices and inverses on compact logarithm
charts give the proper quotient, as in the same lemma.

Apply $V_{\zeta_Ns}$ to the first equation of
\eqref{cdp:eq:C.EW.3} and use \eqref{cdp:eq:C.EW.9}.
The correction $h_jn_{\sigma j}/N$ is a real rational
multiple of an integral vector, so it transports to the
correction in \eqref{cdp:eq:C.EW.13}. The translation is in
the target frame, as expressed by
$\tau_j(s)=V_{\zeta_Ns}\tau_j$. For the order equation,
$R^{N-1-a}V_{\zeta_N^{a+1}s}=V_sR^{N-1-a}$; summing gives
the period $V_s\mu_j$. These are the full overlap and
finite-order equations. Likewise \eqref{cdp:eq:C.EW.6}
gives \eqref{cdp:eq:C.EW.14}.

On each successive component chart subtract the accumulated
edge term $\theta_j(s)$. Its transformed coordinate differs
by \eqref{cdp:eq:C.EW.15}. Changing $j$ changes this by an
integral period, using \eqref{cdp:eq:C.EW.13}; the last edge
adds $B_s$. Changing the logarithm adds an integral normal
vector. Thus these maps descend to a single holomorphic
action on the entire punctured period quotient, with the
already proved order and full affine constants.

Multiplying every vector by $s^{-k}$ gives
\eqref{cdp:eq:C.EW.18} and \eqref{cdp:eq:C.EW.19}.
The image vector is multiplied by $(\zeta_Ns)^{-k}$, so the
linear part is $\rho^{-1}R=J\overline R J^{-1}$ and its
translation is exactly \eqref{cdp:eq:C.EW.20}. These are
the frame changes of the same action, hence preserve every
overlap and order identity. At $w=0$ the frame is
$J\overline{(\cdot)}$; it conjugates the lattice, affine
constants and finite action with the displayed inverse base
rotation. This proves the final comparison.
\end{proof}

